1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,282
As we saw in the last lecture, a major cause of climate change is

2
00:00:06,282 --> 00:00:10,697
the rapid rise in the level of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere over the

3
00:00:10,697 --> 00:00:11,540
last century.

4
00:00:11,540 --> 00:00:17,223
If we could reduce the amount of CO2, perhaps the rate of climate change

5
00:00:17,223 --> 00:00:18,930
could also be slowed down.

6
00:00:18,930 --> 00:00:24,831
One potential method involves enhancing the role of the soil that plants grow in,

7
00:00:24,831 --> 00:00:27,520
with regard to absorbing CO2.

8
00:00:27,520 --> 00:00:34,916
Rattan Lai, a soil scientist from Ohio State University, in the USA, claims that

9
00:00:34,916 --> 00:00:41,009
the world's agricultural soils could potentially absorb 13 percent of the carbon dioxide in

10
00:00:41,009 --> 00:00:46,180
the atmosphere - the equivalent of the amount released in the last 30 years.

11
00:00:46,180 --> 00:00:50,070
And research is going on into how this might be achieved.

12
00:00:50,070 --> 00:00:55,402
Lai first came to the idea that soil might be valuable in this way

13
00:00:55,402 --> 00:00:57,988
not through an interest in climate change,

14
00:00:57,988 --> 00:01:03,100
but rather out of concern for the land itself and the people dependent on

15
00:01:03,100 --> 00:01:03,320
it.

16
00:01:03,320 --> 00:01:08,269
Carbon-rich soil is dark, crumbly and fertile,

17
00:01:08,269 --> 00:01:10,180
and retains some water.

18
00:01:10,180 --> 00:01:13,813
But erosion can occur if soil is dry,

19
00:01:13,813 --> 00:01:18,470
which is a likely effect if it contains inadequate amounts of carbon.

20
00:01:18,470 --> 00:01:23,177
Erosion is of course bad for people trying to grow crops

21
00:01:23,177 --> 00:01:25,470
or breed animals on that terrain.

22
00:01:25,470 --> 00:01:31,289
In the 1970s and '80s, Lai was studying soils in Africa

23
00:01:31,289 --> 00:01:37,650
so devoid of organic matter that the around had become extremely hard, like cement.

24
00:01:37,650 --> 00:01:42,050
There he met a pioneer in the study of global warming,

25
00:01:42,050 --> 00:01:46,630
who suggested that carbon from the soil had moved into the atmosphere.

26
00:01:46,630 --> 00:01:49,775
This is now looking increasingly likely.

27
00:01:49,775 --> 00:01:51,690
Let me explain.

28
00:01:51,690 --> 00:01:58,260
For millions of years, carbon dioxide levels in the atmosphere have been regulated, in

29
00:01:58,260 --> 00:02:04,830
part, by a natural partnership between plants and microbes - tiny organisms in the soil.

30
00:02:04,830 --> 00:02:10,990
Plants absorb CO2 from the air and transform it into sugars

31
00:02:10,990 --> 00:02:13,230
and other carbon-based substances.

32
00:02:13,230 --> 00:02:20,616
While a proportion of these carbon products remain in the plant, some transfer from the roots to fungi

33
00:02:20,616 --> 00:02:21,848
and soil microbes,

34
00:02:21,848 --> 00:02:24,720
which store the carbon in the soil.

35
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:31,749
The invention of agriculture some 10,000 years ago disrupted these ancient soil-building processes

36
00:02:31,749 --> 00:02:36,770
and led to the loss of carbon from the soil.

37
00:02:36,770 --> 00:02:40,060
When humans started draining the natural topsoil,

38
00:02:40,060 --> 00:02:46,170
and ploughing it up for planting, they exposed the buried carbon to oxygen.

39
00:02:46,170 --> 00:02:51,420
This created carbon dioxide and released it into the air.

40
00:02:51,420 --> 00:02:58,626
And in some places, grazing by domesticated animals has removed all vegetation, releasing carbon

41
00:02:58,626 --> 00:03:00,170
into the air.

42
00:03:00,170 --> 00:03:06,082
Tons of carbon have been stripped from the world's soils - where it's needed -

43
00:03:06,082 --> 00:03:08,356
and pumped into the atmosphere.

44
00:03:08,356 --> 00:03:10,630
So what can be done?

45
00:03:10,630 --> 00:03:16,359
Researchers are now coming up with evidence that even modest changes to farming can

46
00:03:16,359 --> 00:03:20,860
significantly help to reduce the amount of carbon in the atmosphere.

47
00:03:20,860 --> 00:03:27,500
Some growers have already started using an approach known as regenerative agriculture.

48
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:34,024
This aims to boost the fertility of soil and keep it moist through established

49
00:03:34,024 --> 00:03:34,490
practices.

50
00:03:34,490 --> 00:03:38,445
These include keeping fields planted all year round,

51
00:03:38,445 --> 00:03:42,400
and increasing the variety of plants being grown.

52
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:48,492
Strategies like these can significantly increase the amount of carbon stored in the soil,

53
00:03:48,492 --> 00:03:54,585
so agricultural researchers are now building a case for their use in combating climate

54
00:03:54,585 --> 00:03:55,020
change.

55
00:03:55,020 --> 00:04:06,047
One American investigation into the potential for storing CO2 on agricultural lands is taking

56
00:04:06,047 --> 00:04:08,410
place in California.

57
00:04:08,410 --> 00:04:15,337
Soil scientist Whendee Silver of the University of California, Berkeley, is conducting a first-of-its-kind

58
00:04:15,337 --> 00:04:19,790
study on a large cattle farm in the state.

59
00:04:19,790 --> 00:04:26,192
She and her students are testing the effects on carbon storage of the compost

60
00:04:26,192 --> 00:04:31,222
that is created from waste - both agricultural, including manure and cornstalks,

61
00:04:31,222 --> 00:04:35,338
and waste produced in gardens, such as leaves, branches,

62
00:04:35,338 --> 00:04:36,710
and lawn trimmings.

63
00:04:36,710 --> 00:04:45,040
In Australia, soil ecologist Christine Jones is testing another promising soil-enrichment strategy.

64
00:04:45,040 --> 00:04:51,837
Jones and 12 farmers are working to build up soil carbon by cultivating grasses

65
00:04:51,837 --> 00:04:54,750
that stay green all year round.

66
00:04:54,750 --> 00:04:58,682
Like composting, the approach has already been proved experimentally;

67
00:04:58,682 --> 00:05:04,361
Jones now hopes to show that it can be applied on working farms

68
00:05:04,361 --> 00:05:08,730
and that the resulting carbon capture can be accurately measured.

69
00:05:08,730 --> 00:05:15,114
It's hoped in the future that projects such as these will demonstrate the role that farmers

70
00:05:15,114 --> 00:05:20,700
and other land managers can play in reducing the harmful effects of greenhouse gases.

71
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:27,513
For example, in countries like the United States, where most farming operations use large

72
00:05:27,513 --> 00:05:33,840
applications of fertiliser, changing such long-standing habits will require a change of system.

73
00:05:33,840 --> 00:05:38,942
Rattan Lai argues that farmers should receive payment not just for the corn

74
00:05:38,942 --> 00:05:40,512
or beef they produce,

75
00:05:40,512 --> 00:05:44,830
but also for the carbon they can store in their soil.

76
00:05:44,830 --> 00:05:49,000
Another study being carried out ...
